  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dense fog this morning, then sunny and warm\n\nIn the wake of yesterday's warm front, increasing dewpoints and\nclear skies have led to areas of dense fog this morning.\nNighttime microphysics imagery vividly depicts fog-filled\nvalleys across Central PA, prompting the issuance of a Dense \nFog Advisory until 14Z/10AM. Meanwhile, an area of low clouds\nhas developed in the Lower Susquehanna Valley extending along\nand just northwest of the I-95 corridor. The latest model\nguidance indicates that this cloud cover could be rather\nstubborn today, perhaps not fully eroding until early afternoon. \n\nOnce the fog mixes out and clouds lift, a mostly sunny afternoon\nis on tap. Temperatures will soar into the mid 60s to low 70s,\nwhich is anywhere from 10 to 20 degrees above normal for this\ntime of year. It could be a good day to rake leaves, though many\nmore leaves will fall tonight and tomorrow with windy conditions\nexpected.\n\nNOTE: October 29th is the anniversary of two historic weather \nevents to impact central Pennsylvania in the past 15 years: \nSandy in 2012 and the Snowstorm in 2011. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Fire weather concerns increasing by Friday \n\nIt has been almost two weeks since we've received any measurable\nrainfall across Central PA. A cold front will sweep through the\narea from northwest to southeast overnight tonight into Friday\nmorning. There could be a few light showers in northwest PA but\ndry conditions are expected for most. The cold front will bring\ngusty northwest winds and much lower dewpoints. The combination\nof drying fuels, increasing leaf litter, and low humidity air \ncould result in an elevated risk of wildfire spread Friday and \nSaturday. Friday will be breezy in the wake of a cold front with\nmarginally low afternoon RH values and max gusts forecast in \nthe 20-25 mph range. That will result in an elevated risk of\nwildfire spread and plenty of falling leaves (and perhaps a\nrapid decline in fall color). PA Bureau of Forestry partners \nhave indicated fuels are now sufficiently dry to support the \nissuance of a wildfire spread statement /if needed/ for Friday.\nAlthough relative humidity values will remain low through the\nweekend, lighter winds should limit the risk of rapid wildfire\nspread. Even still, individuals should limit activities that\ncould start a fire. 99% of wildfires in Pennsylvania are caused\nby humans!\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Rain-free and mild conditions into early \nNovember\n\nThe latest model guidance indicates the lengthy dry spell will \nlast through Halloween and probably into the first few days of \nNovember. There appears to be a modest multi-model/ensemble \nsignal building toward the possibility of a frontal type heavy \nrain/wind event sometime during the second half of next week \naround the Nov. 5-7th timeframe. The Climate Prediction Center \nhas highlighted all of Pennsylvania in a Slight Risk of High \nWinds for 11/5, noting the potential for a strong low pressure \nsystem to traverse the Great Lake region and bring gusty winds \nto the region. There is also a Slight Risk of Heavy \nPrecipitation for southeast PA on 11/5. We'll need to monitor \nthe potential for some rainfall, though hopefully most of it \nwill be beneficial!",
